Starting with just the empty subset, each number can be added to every subset that already exists. The new copies are collected before the next number is processed.

This avoids bit operations and is easy to follow. For every input value, the number of stored subsets doubles.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> powerSet(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        result.add(new ArrayList<>());

        for (int value : nums) {
            int oldSize = result.size();

            for (int i = 0; i < oldSize; i++) {
                List<Integer> nextSubset = new ArrayList<>(result.get(i));
                nextSubset.add(value);
                result.add(nextSubset);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Dry run with `[1, 2]`:

- Start with `[[]]`
- Add `1`: `[[], [1]]`
- Add `2`: `[[], [1], [2], [1, 2]]`

The `oldSize` variable is important. It prevents the loop from adding the current value repeatedly to subsets created during the same round.

Time complexity: `O(n * 2^n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` for the list of returned subsets.

Gray code changes only one bit between two nearby masks. Using it is not required to generate the subsets, but it gives an interesting order where consecutive subsets differ by one element.

For each number from `0` to `2^n - 1`, the Gray code mask is `i ^ (i >> 1)`. The mask is then read in the same way as a normal bitmask.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int> > powerSet(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int total = 1 << n;
        vector<vector<int> > result;

        for (int value = 0; value < total; value++) {
            int mask = value ^ (value >> 1);
            vector<int> subset;

            for (int bit = 0; bit < n; bit++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << bit)) != 0) {
                    subset.push_back(nums[bit]);
                }
            }

            result.push_back(subset);
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

The empty array has one mask, `0`, so it returns one empty subset. A one-element array returns the two expected subsets, the empty one and the one containing that value.

This keeps the input unchanged. The main difference from counting masks directly is only the order of the returned subsets.

Time complexity: `O(n * 2^n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` for the output.

Each array value gives two choices. The recursive call first keeps the value, then removes it and explores the branch where the value is skipped. Once the index reaches the end, the current list is a complete subset.

The saved lists must be new `ArrayList` objects. Otherwise, later backtracking would also change subsets already stored in the answer.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> powerSet(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        makeSubsets(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private void makeSubsets(int[] nums, int index, List<Integer> current,
                             List<List<Integer>> result) {
        if (index == nums.length) {
            result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
            return;
        }

        current.add(nums[index]);
        makeSubsets(nums, index + 1, current, result);
        current.remove(current.size() - 1);

        makeSubsets(nums, index + 1, current, result);
    }
}
```

Dry run for `[4, 6]` starts with two branches for `4`: keep it or skip it. Each branch then does the same thing for `6`, producing `[[4, 6], [4], [6], []]`.

For `nums = []`, the first call is already at the end and returns `[[]]`. The input array itself is never modified.

Time complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` including the cost of copying each finished subset.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the current path and recursion stack, excluding the output list.

A mask gives one simple way to decide which values belong to each subset. Bit `j` tells whether `nums[j]` should be included. The all-zero mask creates the empty subset.

For example, with `[1, 2]`, mask `01` gives `[1]`, mask `10` gives `[2]`, and mask `11` gives `[1, 2]`.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int> > powerSet(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int totalMasks = 1 << n;
        vector<vector<int> > result;

        for (int mask = 0; mask < totalMasks; mask++) {
            vector<int> subset;

            for (int bit = 0; bit < n; bit++) {
                if (mask & (1 << bit)) {
                    subset.push_back(nums[bit]);
                }
            }

            result.push_back(subset);
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Dry run for `nums = [5, 7]`:

- `00` gives `[]`
- `01` gives `[5]`
- `10` gives `[7]`
- `11` gives `[5, 7]`

There are `2^n` masks, so the empty input still correctly returns one subset, `[]`.

Time complexity: `O(n * 2^n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` for the returned subsets, excluding the small loop variables.
